---
import ArticleLayout from "@/layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const evidence =
  "https://github.com/mimic-browser/runtime/blob/main/benchmark/runs/13-rss-20260929/public-summary.md";
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="Mimic versus Chromium for repeated JavaScript extraction"
  eyebrow="Choosing a runtime"
  description="Choose a separate Mimic runtime lane for supported nonvisual HTML and JSON extraction, with measured memory evidence and a Chromium fallback."
>
  <p>
    If your job repeatedly runs JavaScript to extract HTML or JSON, its result
    may not need rendered pixels. Mimic offers a separate runtime lane for those
    supported workloads: V8 execution, browser state and CDP without embedding
    Chromium. Chromium remains the full browser option. Mimic is a
    source-available public beta with partial CDP and Web API compatibility.
  </p>
  <h2>Choose by the output and required browser behavior</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Workload requirement</th><th>Runtime choice</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr
        ><td>Plain HTTP response, with no browser JavaScript or state needed</td
        ><td
          >Start with an HTTP client; a browser runtime may be unnecessary.</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>Repeated DOM or JavaScript extraction producing HTML or JSON</td
        ><td
          >Evaluate Mimic if the required CDP commands and Web APIs are
          supported.</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>Rendered screenshots, video, PDF or full Blink behavior</td><td
          >Use Chromium; Mimic does not supply these rendering capabilities.</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>Unknown site behavior or unsupported browser features</td><td
          >Keep a Chromium fallback and validate the extracted result.</td
        ></tr
      >
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Reading DOM HTML or structured values from JavaScript is different from
    proving that a page looks correct. Mimic's modeled DOM, style and geometry
    observations are bounded; they do not replace full browser rendering.
  </p>
  <h2>Memory evidence from one controlled run</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href={evidence}>September 29, 2026 benchmark</a> compared a particular
    Mimic development executable with Chrome 152.0.7977.82 in headless mode on Windows
    11, an Intel i7-14700KF and 31.83 GiB RAM. These observations are not release-wide
    promises or a measurement of your production workload.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead
      ><tr><th>Measured metric</th><th>Mimic</th><th>Chrome 152</th></tr></thead
    >
    <tbody>
      <tr
        ><td>Ready process-tree RSS</td><td>45.79 MiB</td><td>379.54 MiB</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>Active RSS, completed 50 static Pages series</td><td>748.11 MiB</td
        ><td>4102.04 MiB</td></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>Throughput, same 50 static Pages series</td><td
          >108.67 sessions/s</td
        ><td>18.09 sessions/s</td></tr
      >
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    RSS sums the process tree and can count shared pages more than once; ready
    RSS is not incremental Page memory. Throughput includes setup and teardown.
    The binary was built from changes later committed as <code>04ac211</code>,
    with SHA-256 <code
      >db82f1c85faca672e7225260e4d0cc603d6a867effb7ca60a540e9e14eceabc4</code
    >. It is not automatically the v0.1.9 release binary.
  </p>
  <p>
    All 12 correctness gates and 360 measured single-page attempts passed in
    these fixtures. Six concurrency series stopped or contained a failure;
    stopped levels are not successful scaling results. Chrome was faster in
    multiple warm completion workloads, including DOM mutations and React. These
    data do not establish general website compatibility, universal speed gains
    or a lower infrastructure bill. See <a href={`${base}benchmarks/`}
      >the full benchmark context</a
    >.
  </p>
  <h2>Use a separate lane with a Chrome fallback</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Define a result contract: required fields, acceptable HTML, errors and
      session boundaries.
    </li>
    <li>
      Validate a representative set of target pages against the same contract in
      Mimic and Chrome.
    </li>
    <li>
      Route proven nonvisual jobs to a separate Mimic worker pool. Independent
      Pages and Contexts allow isolated jobs; choose concurrency from
      measurements.
    </li>
    <li>
      Route jobs needing rendering or unsupported behavior to Chromium. Record
      why a job falls back; do not silently accept incomplete extraction.
    </li>
    <li>
      Measure total completion time, CPU, peak memory, failure rate and fallback
      rate before changing capacity.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    If replaying a job could repeat a purchase, submission or other side effect,
    design that retry explicitly. The fallback is a routing policy you build,
    not an automatic feature promised by Mimic. Site rules and normal access
    controls still apply.
  </p>
  <p>
    Begin with <a href={`${base}playwright-without-chromium/`}
      >the runnable Playwright guide</a
    >{" "}
    and check <a href={`${base}docs/#compatibility`}>compatibility boundaries</a
    >{" "}
    before moving a production workload.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
